===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*speḱ-}}
{{etymon|en|id=aim|:der|it:scopo<t:purpose>|tree=1}}
From {{der|en|it|scopo||purpose}}, from {{der|en|la|scopus||target}},<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref><ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster Online}}</ref> from {{der|en|grc|σκοπός}}, from {{m|grc|σκέπτομαι}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*speḱ-}}. Etymologically related to {{m|en|skeptic}} and {{m|en|spectrum}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}
[[File:4x rifle scope.jpg|thumb|4x rifle '''scope''']]

# {{senseid|en|domain of subject matter}} The [[breadth]], [[depth]] or [[reach]] of a subject; the [[extent]] of [[applicability]] or [[relevance]]; a [[domain]], [[purview]] or [[remit#Noun|remit]].
#: {{syn|en|compass<id:scope><qq:formal>|ambit}}
#: {{uxi|en|Environmental impacts lie outside the '''scope''' of this report.}}
#: {{uxi|en|Such transactions fall within the '''scope''' of VAT.}}
# {{lb|en|weapons}} A device used in aiming a [[projectile]], through which the person aiming looks at the intended target.
#: {{syn|en|telescopic sight}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Sgt. Jack Coughlin; Donald A. Davis|title=On Scope: A Sniper Novel|publisher=St. Martin's Press|isbn=9781250037923
|passage=Coastie yanked her eye away from the night '''scope''' when those big lights were caught by it and amplified in intensity. Her entire view had gone white in an instant. “I can't see!” Temporarily blinded, she let touch become her primary sense, dropped the M40, and grabbed her alternate weapon, an M16 with an ACOG day '''scope''' that was already registered for the same distance.}}
# Potential range of action; degree of freedom; opportunity.
#: {{uxi|en|My job doesn't give me much '''scope''' for personal development.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2001|author=Mike Hughes; Andy Vass|title=Strategies for Closing the Learning Gap|page=19
|passage=It is also true that the vast majority of teachers are highly skilled and experienced professionals who are already doing an excellent job in the classroom, thus leaving relatively little '''scope''' for improvement.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Mary Kitt-Neel|title=Lie Down in Princess Position
|passage=She had in fact put in a resume at another firm that gave their graphics team much more '''scope'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=|title=Balijatra to celebrate classical Odia language, its authors in 2025|journal=[[w:The New Indian Express|The New Indian Express]]|date=2025-10-10|url=https://www.newindianexpress.com/states/odisha/2025/Oct/10/balijatra-to-celebrate-classical-odia-language-its-authors-in-2025|passage=VISITORS to Balijatra will get a '''scope''' to learn about the history and richness of classical Odia language and writers who contributed towards enriching it.}}
# {{lb|en|programming}} The region of program [[source code]] in which a given [[identifier]] is meaningful, or a given object can be accessed.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Mary Campione; Kathy Walrath; Alison Huml|title=The Java Tutorial: A Short Course on the Basics|publisher=Addison-Wesley Professional|isbn=9780201703931|page=72
|passage=A variable's '''scope''' is the region of a program within which the variable can be referred to by its simple name. Secondarily, '''scope''' also determines when the system creates and destroys memory for the variable. '''Scope''' is distinct from visibility, which applies only to member variables and determines whether the variable can be used from outside of the class within which it is declared.}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} The shortest [[sub-]][[wff]] of which a given instance of a [[logical connective]] is a part.
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} The region of an [[utterance]] to which some modifying element applies.
#: {{ux|en|the '''scope''' of an adverb}}
# {{senseid|en|ellipsis of optic instrument names}} ''Ellipsis of any word ending in [[-scope]], such as [[endoscope]], [[periscope]], [[telescope]], [[microscope]], [[oscilloscope]], and so on.''
# {{senseid|en|ellipsis of endoscopy or the names of types thereof}} {{lb|en|medicine|colloquial}} Any [[medical]] [[procedure]] that ends in the suffix {{m|en|-scopy}}, such as {{m|en|endoscopy}}, {{m|en|colonoscopy}}, {{m|en|bronchoscopy}}, etc.

